"""Individually authored T76 mechanism cards; authoring is not learner acceptance."""
from author_replicated_support import materialize

def card(title,case,census,refs,claim,problem,construct,example,evidence,transfer,analysis,hints,terms,costs,imports):
    return dict(title=title,case=case,census=census,refs=['R-RP-'+s for s in refs],claim=claim,problem=problem,construct=construct,example=example,evidence=evidence,transfer=transfer,analysis=analysis,hints=hints,terms=terms,costs=costs,imports=imports,families='ABCDEFGHIJ')

DATA=[
card('Positioned event logs and persistent snapshot roots','log',['event-sourcing','memory-image'],['EVENTS','CRDT'],
 'Distinguish immutable event history, a current projection, a retained snapshot root and durable storage. Derive why replay requires event-version and external-query assumptions, not merely a list of bytes.',
 'Implement RPLog and rp_log_init/append/get. The original in-memory log contains at most16 events (position1..16,key0..31,int32 value) and17 snapshot roots. Each append first updates the imported persist.hamt arena, then publishes the event and root together. get(position,key) queries the exact retained prefix; position0 is empty. Invalid key/position, log full and injected HAMT allocation failure preserve published state and output. Replay an admitted prefix into a fresh fixed-address arena; values are latest event payload per key, not accumulated balances. No filesystem durability is implied.',
 'Retain the actual PR-T70-07 learner artifact and its64node/3nodes-per-update contract. Prove16 appends use48nodes, every published root remains immutable and an interrupted failed append cannot advance count. Initialization requires no surviving borrowed roots; do not copy/relocate a live arena and interpret roots in another owner. Account for unpublished cells and allocation counters separately from reachable state. State a release/publication order for a future concurrent adapter.',
 'Append(3,7),append(3,9): prefix1 returns7 and prefix2 returns9. Inject each allocation ordinal1..3 on the next append: count stays2, used count is restored and prior roots still resolve. The17th append returns RP_FULL without consuming nodes.',
 'Run replicated.log and the three allocation-cut assertions, compare every prefix against an independent key/value dictionary and rebuild into a fresh arena. Reject count-before-commit and latest-root-for-all-snapshots mutants. Inspect source/link dependencies to establish that the real imported HAMT is used. Separately implement the required codec.records/storage.pages adapter with short writes and crash schedules; the memory anchor does not validate that adapter.',
 'Replace the in-memory append with versioned checksummed records on the prior storage.pages artifact; enumerate every tear/flush prefix and recover the longest committed prefix. Persist snapshots with their event position and schema identity. Demonstrate why replaying an external effect twice or using a changed external query result changes semantics; retain original responses as events where required.',
 'The HAMT update either publishes a new root or leaves the old reachable graph intact. Publish log count only after update success. Arena capacity is a semantic limit: silently recycling nodes would invalidate snapshots. A durable variant needs ordering, torn-record detection and a commit model stated by the imported storage component. Fowler describes replay concerns, not a proof that fsync makes arbitrary multi-file writes atomic.',
 ['Write the invariant linking count, events and roots.','Inject failure before each of the three path clones.','A retained root is a memory snapshot, not a durable transaction.'],
 [('event position','A stable ordinal within one identified immutable log history.','wall-clock timestamp','Clock values can tie or move backward and do not identify a contiguous prefix.'),('projection snapshot root','A retained immutable root describing state after a particular event prefix.','durable checkpoint','Memory reachability alone survives neither process loss nor storage corruption.')],
 'Inline16events and17roots plus the imported65node arena; append clones3nodes, lookup has bounded trie depth, replay O(events). Retained roots pin all prior published nodes; no allocator or reclamation occurs. Publication and persistence costs are distinct.', ['persist.hamt','codec.records','storage.pages','testing.faults']),
card('CQRS projection admission and atomic position advancement','projection',['cqrs'],['CQRS','EVENTS'],
 'Separate query-model choice from delivery semantics. Establish exactly-once application only within one serialized projection and one retained immutable log identity; reject the stronger claim of exactly-once external effects.',
 'Implement RPProjection with sixteen retained applied events, a contiguous count and int64 total of int32 event values. Admit positions1..16 in order. A byte-independent semantic duplicate (same position,key,value) succeeds without mutation; conflicting payload at an old position returns RP_CONFLICT; a gap returns RP_GAP. Publish total, retained event and cursor as one serialized transition. Supplied pthread adapter holds one mutex across this entire call; two threads each deliver the same ordered1..16 stream.',
 'Prove the invariant count=k implies exactly the first k events contributed once and applied[i].position=i+1. A 16-term int32 sum fits int64 on the declared target. Retain history for conflict checks rather than trusting a reused position from a new log. The caller must associate a projection with one log identity. Explain why a mutex around cursor alone is insufficient, and why an external email/file effect cannot become atomic with this state by naming it CQRS.',
 'Apply(1,0,7) twice yields total7,count1. Reuse position1 with8 conflicts; position3 before2 is a gap. Two synchronized threads each delivering values1..16 yield count16,total136;128 fresh concurrent runs test the adapter.',
 'Run replicated.projection, including actual pthread schedules under GCC TSan when available. Kill duplicate-reapplication and gap-acceptance mutants with semantic assertions. Record mutex boundaries and model the crash point between query-state and cursor persistence. A TSan pass observes these executions only; it does not certify arbitrary effects or all schedules.',
 'Add a durable inbox/outbox with log UUID and idempotency keys using storage.pages and ipc.local. Enumerate crash/retry points on both sides of a local synthetic receiver. Produce one duplicate-side-effect counterexample without receiver deduplication and show the residual requirement for atomic receiver state. Compare rebuildable read models with authoritative event history.',
 'The operation checks old positions before changing total. Conflicts are semantic, not a raw-struct memcmp that depends on padding. Serialized duplicates do not add again. In the threaded harness, each thread has an increasing stream, so their union is always a prefix under the lock. Cursor and total persistence need a shared commit protocol; otherwise recovery can skip or repeat work.',
 ['Write the prefix invariant before choosing a cursor type.','A duplicate must refer to the same event, not only the same integer.','CQRS separates models; it does not supply delivery or storage atomicity.'],
 [('projection application identity','The combination of log identity, event position and immutable event content used to admit a duplicate.','transport delivery identity','A new delivery may carry an already applied event.'),('projection commit boundary','The atomic transition joining query-state change and its progress cursor.','cursor-only synchronization','Protecting a cursor while racing the state does not preserve the projection invariant.')],
 'Sixteen semantic event copies plus count and int64 total; O(1) admission, retained history bounded to16, no allocation. Mutex contention and total adapter latency must be measured separately from pure admission. Durable inbox/outbox costs are additional.', ['storage.pages','ipc.local','testing.faults']),
card('Lamport, vector and hybrid logical clock boundaries','clocks',['logical-clock'],['LAMPORT','HLC'],
 'Derive three different clock contracts: Lamport timestamps preserve happened-before in one direction, vectors characterize causality under fixed known membership, and HLC pairs retain a physical component without eliminating physical-clock assumptions.',
 'Implement checked Lamport receive max(local,remote)+1, fixed3-replica vector merge-then-increment and partial-order comparison (-1 before,0 equal,1 after,2 concurrent). Implement HLC Figure5 using uint64 physical and uint32 logical components, local/send events via remote=NULL and receive events via a remote pair. Apply every max/tie branch; backwards wall time is allowed. Counter overflow returns RP_RANGE with output unchanged; do not wrap and silently violate causality.',
 'Show two concurrent events with ordered Lamport timestamps, two incomparable vectors and every HLC equality case. Separate physical-clock closeness claims from the synchronization assumptions in Corollary1. On local overflow choose explicit failure; neither arbitrary sleep nor silently increasing physical time is equivalent. Outputs are ordinary C structs, so validity and per-replica serialization remain caller obligations.',
 'Lamport(2,8)=9. Vectors[1,0,0] and[0,1,0] are concurrent; receive at replica0 gives[2,1,0]. HLC local(10,2),remote(10,7),wall9 gives(10,8); wall11 on a subsequent local event resets logical to0. UINT32_MAX logical overflow fails unchanged.',
 'Run replicated.clocks and independent exhaustive small HLC branch enumeration plus uint64/uint32 extrema. Kill ignoring-remote Lamport and incorrect equal-physical HLC-counter mutants. Build small message histories and derive causal relations from graph reachability before comparing clock outputs; timestamp ordering alone is not the oracle.',
 'Change replica membership and persisted restart identities; show why resetting a vector slot or reusing a timestamp identity loses history. Add a bounded clock-skew model and prove exactly which HLC closeness bound follows. Compare scalar timestamp wire size, vector growth, and a protocol that waits versus rejects on finite counter exhaustion.',
 'HLC first chooses max(local physical,remote physical,wall), then increments the maximum counter among sources tied at that physical value, or resets0 if wall alone wins. The implementation rejects counter overflow before publishing. The vector model assumes a fixed3-member universe and unique serialized event counters; process restart and dynamic membership require a new identity protocol.',
 ['Enumerate which physical sources tie for the maximum.','Keep physical selection separate from logical-counter selection.','Timestamp order is not the converse of causal order.'],
 [('happened-before order','The transitive relation generated by local event order and message send-before-receive.','total timestamp order','Tie-breaking concurrent events creates order without creating causality.'),('HLC physical maximum','The largest physical component known through local wall observations and received histories.','synchronized wall time','A causal maximum alone does not establish a bound relative to true time.')],
 'Lamport8bytes, vector3uint64 values, HLCuint64+uint32 plus ABI padding; no dynamic storage. Updates O(1) for fixed membership, O(replicas) generally. Persisted counters and clock reads are separate costs; the C core performs no system clock calls.', ['codec.records','testing.faults']),
card('Join-semilattice counters and deterministic registers','counters',['conflict-free-replicated-data-type-crdt'],['CRDT','DELTA'],
 'Derive convergence from explicit componentwise joins and globally unique writer identities, and distinguish state merge from replaying operations. A last-writer register resolves an order chosen by the protocol, not necessarily real-time recency.',
 'Implement RPPN with3 positive and3 negative uint32 components, checked local increments and int64 query sum(P)-sum(N). Merge by componentwise maximum, allowing self-merge. Implement RPRegister merge by lexicographic(timestamp,replica0..2); identical identity with different payload is RP_CONFLICT. A canonical initial register is(0,0,0), and generated writes use positive timestamps. Inputs must derive from one writer per replica identity; public fields are not Byzantine proofs.',
 'Prove idempotence, commutativity and associativity on admitted compatible states, plus inflation of each grow-only component. Explain why sum of received states double-counts retransmission, why finite overflow must reject, and why a reused writer identity with forked state violates the ownership model. For registers identify conflict cases outside the unique-event assumption and the total-order tie rule.',
 'Replica0 adds5; replica1 subtracts3. Merge yields2 and repeating the same merge remains2. Register(7,0,1) joined with(7,2,9) chooses9; another payload at(7,2) is a conflict. Adding UINT32_MAX to component5 fails unchanged.',
 'Run replicated.counters; enumerate small component states and three-way merge permutations, including duplicate, reordered and dropped-then-retransmitted messages. Kill additive-state-merge and missing replica tie-break mutants. Show a counterexample for unchecked uint32 wrap and for independent writers sharing one replica slot.',
 'Add membership replacement, serialized snapshots and state corruption detection. Distinguish crash fault tolerance from Byzantine tolerance: demonstrate a synthetic forged high component monopolizing the merge and specify an authenticated admission/identity policy without claiming signatures solve semantic authorization. Compare state size with operation-log retention under the same delivery assumptions.',
 'Component max is a join on the bounded product order while updates stay within range. The register operation is convergent only for compatible unique-identity events; reporting a conflicting reuse prevents silently inventing a winner. The test set is finite; the algebraic argument and changed membership case remain separately assessed.',
 ['Write the partial order before the merge routine.','Try receiving exactly the same state twice.','A unique replica label is an ownership obligation, not a property of an unsigned field.'],
 [('state join idempotence','Joining an admitted state with itself leaves it unchanged.','operation replay','Reapplying a non-idempotent increment can change the result.'),('register arbitration stamp','A protocol-defined totally ordered event identity selecting a value.','real-time last write','The stamp order need not match physical occurrence order.')],
 'Sixuint32 components and a timestamp/replica/value register; fixed O(3) merge, no allocation/drop. State traffic grows with membership. Logical deletion cannot shrink history without a separately proven protocol.', ['codec.records','io.frame']),
card('Observed-remove sets and replayable delta state','sets',['delta-state-crdt'],['CRDT','DELTA'],
 'Explain add-wins observed removal through unique add identities, retained tombstones and idempotent delta joins. Distinguish a causally self-contained delta representation from arbitrary partial operations or unsafe tombstone collection.',
 'Implement RPSet for values0..3 and2 replica writers, each with8 never-reused add identities. adds[value] and removed[value] are16-bit tag sets; next[replica] reserves the next local identity. add returns a delta containing its new tag and counter frontier; remove returns tombstones only for tags actually observed. Merge ORs add/tombstone sets and takes frontier maxima. Empty removal succeeds. Exhausted identity space returns RP_FULL; no reset/reuse while any state/delta survives.',
 'Define membership as adds minus removed. Derive why a concurrent unobserved add survives removal and why an observed add cannot resurrect when its delta arrives again. Treat one writer per replica as a premise. The bounded delta carries exact tag tombstones, so remove-before-add delivery is representable; do not infer this for all causal CRDTs. Explain the delta-interval causal precondition from the paper and specify acknowledgment/stability requirements before reclaiming history.',
 'Replica0 adds value2; replica1 observes it. Replica0 removes while replica1 concurrently adds its own tag. Join retains value2. Removing after observing both tags and replaying either old add delta does not resurrect it. The ninth add by one replica fails without resetting its identity counter.',
 'Run replicated.sets and enumerate two-writer add/remove/delivery traces against a mathematical tagged-set oracle. Include duplicate and remove-before-add delivery. Kill remove-all-possible-tags and omitted-tombstone-merge mutants. Inventory retained tags and show the exact full condition rather than silently dropping history.',
 'Introduce a third replica, disconnection and crash/restart. Design a causal delta-interval anti-entropy protocol with durable incarnation/counter identity and full-state fallback; falsify premature garbage collection with a delayed old delta. Prove a stable frontier before discarding tombstones, or retain them and quantify growth.',
 'A remove records observed identities, not the value globally and not future possible identities. The original bitset model retains all16 identities, making merge closure and reordering easy to inspect. It does not implement dynamic membership or unbounded tombstone reclamation. Deltas are state fragments joined idempotently; treating them as a list of add/remove commands changes semantics.',
 ['Give every add a writer and local sequence identity.','Deliver the removal tombstone before its matching add.','Deleting tombstones is not ordinary memory cleanup; delayed messages can resurrect state.'],
 [('observed removal','Removal of the particular add identities visible to a replica.','remove-wins value deletion','A remove-wins policy can suppress a concurrent unseen addition.'),('causal delta interval','A joined range of deltas whose receiver must satisfy the required predecessor-state condition.','arbitrary message subset','An arbitrary subset may omit the causal context required by another CRDT.')],
 'Eightuint16 tag masks plus2counters; O(4) merge, O(1) local operation, no allocation. At most16 add identities lifetime; removed tags remain stored. Wire and acknowledgment costs differ from local bitset cost.', ['codec.records','io.frame','testing.faults']),
card('Replicated sequence identities and contextual transformation','sequence',['operational-transformation'],['CRDT','OT'],
 'Separate stable sequence identity from transient visible position, and compare a bounded parent-tree sequence with context-dependent operational transformation. Pairwise insertion agreement is a necessary local property, not a complete OT correctness theorem.',
 'Implement RPSequence with16 nodes{id,parent,byte,tombstone}. id is globally unique and positive, parent<id; parent0 is the root and every nonzero parent must already exist. Concurrent siblings render in descending ID order by depth-first traversal; tombstoned parents remain traversable. Duplicate identity with identical parent/value is idempotent; conflicting reuse fails. Missing dependencies return RP_GAP unchanged. Render stages output and fails transactionally if capacity is insufficient. Also implement two-concurrent-insert position transformation with distinct priority tie-breaks; no full network OT control algorithm is supplied.',
 'Derive acyclicity from parent<id and closure from causal admission. Prove insertion-order-independent rendering for a fixed admitted node set, including descendants of deleted anchors. Explain that this original tree contract is RGA-inspired, not a claim to implement every detail of Specification19. For OT derive the two-branch diamond under distinct priorities and identify the state-vector/log context needed beyond it.',
 'Insert A(id1,parent0),B(id2,parent0),C(id3,parent1) in either admissible arrival order: render BAC. Delete id1: render BC, retaining descendant C. Two inserts at position0 with priorities1 and2 transform the higher-priority-number insertion to position1.',
 'Run replicated.sequence; enumerate topological arrival orders and deletion subsets against a recursive tree oracle. Kill arrival-order sibling rendering and deleted-subtree omission mutants. Check full capacity, duplicate identity, missing dependency and output failure atomicity. Enumerate both paths of the two-insert OT diamond, then provide a three-operation context counterexample to an unjustified generalization.',
 'Add delete operations delivered before insert, Unicode scalar payloads and snapshot compaction. Compare an explicit pending-dependency queue with tombstone admission. Implement an OT context-control protocol with state vectors and retained transformations, identify assumptions about fixed membership and failures, and test more than pairwise transformation equations.',
 'Stable IDs make tree membership independent of current displayed indices. Descending sibling traversal is deterministic and parent edges remain after deletion. RGA literature uses a particular linked-list timestamp rule; this bounded parent-tree exercise states its own semantics instead of borrowing an unproved equivalence. OT positions refer to an operation context; transforming against the wrong history can converge to an unintended document.',
 ['Do not delete the parent edge when hiding a character.','Sort siblings by identity before recursively rendering descendants.','Two correct transformed insertions do not establish a complete distributed editor.'],
 [('sequence anchor identity','A persistent node identifier used to locate insertion context after visible edits.','visible character offset','Offsets change when concurrent insertions or removals occur.'),('transformation context','The document version and causal history relative to which an operation was defined.','operation syntax','Equal position/value fields can mean different actions in different contexts.')],
 'Sixteen inline nodes; insertion O(n), simple rendering O(n squared) and recursion depth<=16; no heap or reclamation. Tombstones retain anchor topology. Production balancing, Unicode and compacted histories require new contracts and measurements.', ['codec.records','persist.hamt']),
card('Merkle commitments and bounded anti-entropy','merkle',['merkle-tree','content-addressable-storage'],['MERKLE','SHA'],
 'Derive a domain-separated Merkle root for an exact ordered leaf sequence, distinguishing hash equality under a collision assumption from identity authentication, availability or a proof that arbitrary bytes are well formed.',
 'Implement local SHA-256 for byte lengths0..65 directly from RFC6234 equations, and RFC9162-style Merkle roots over0..8 uint32 leaves encoded big-endian. Empty root is SHA256(empty), leaf is SHA256(00||four bytes), internal node is SHA256(01||left||right), split at largest power of two strictly below count. Implement recursive equal-subtree skipping and return a bitmask of differing leaf positions for equal-length trees. No target hash library, network service or certificate-transparency implementation may replace these mechanisms.',
 'Derive padding across55/56/63/64/65-byte boundaries, uint32 modular arithmetic, round constants and endian conversion. Prove the tree shape for non-power-of-two counts and that a subtree comparison is meaningful only with the same position/range/domain. Account for hash collision and corrupt-peer assumptions; a matching root alone authenticates no sender and guarantees no retrievability.',
 'SHA256(empty)=e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855. Trees[1,2,3] and[1,2,9] differ only at bit2 (mask4); empty trees give mask0. Compare every count0..8 with a separately implemented Python hashlib construction.',
 'Run replicated.merkle, standard empty/abc hash vectors, all padding boundaries and independent randomized hashlib differential cases. Kill omitted leaf-domain prefix and ignored right-child mutants. Inspect the generated code for the intended unsigned operations; benchmark nodes hashed versus full leaf comparison under equal correctness/security contracts.',
 'Add inclusion proofs with explicit leaf index/count, verify malformed/truncated/extra paths, then implement anti-entropy over a synthetic local framed transport. Vary chunking and domain identifiers and show root incompatibility. Model a malicious peer returning a missing or inconsistent node; fail without publishing partial repaired state. Do not claim collision resistance from a finite test suite.',
 'The bounded SHA helper needs at most two64-byte blocks; fixed arrays and unsigned rotates avoid signed shifts and allocation. Domain bytes distinguish leaves from interior nodes. The comparison skips only hashes of identical ranges, recursively inspecting changed subtrees. Cryptographic assumptions and implementation validation are separate: differential tests can find bugs but do not prove collision resistance.',
 ['Derive the split for counts3,5 and8 explicitly.','The branch input is65bytes and crosses a SHA padding block boundary.','Content addressing does not authenticate who supplied content.'],
 [('Merkle domain separation','Distinct input encodings for leaf and interior-node hash computations.','hash algorithm choice','A strong hash does not repair ambiguous structural encodings.'),('anti-entropy range commitment','A hash tied to an exact ordered range and tree shape used to locate divergence.','whole-system consistency','Equal range commitments do not establish availability, authorized history or concurrent update ordering.')],
 'Bounded stack buffers128bytes padding plus64uint32 schedule per SHA call; recursive tree depth<=3 for8leaves, O(n) full root, changed-range search retains hash costs. No heap. Measure actual stack with inlining and account for repeated subtree hashing in this inspectable implementation.', ['codec.records','io.frame']),
card('Field tags, minimal varints and canonical bytes','wire',['schema-evolution-via-field-tags','varint','zigzag-encoding','canonical-serialization'],['WIRE','MERKLE'],
 'Distinguish a wire grammar from one canonical encoding and show why deterministic serialization under one binary is not a universal content identity. Derive portable signed ZigZag arithmetic without signed-left-shift assumptions.',
 'Implement RPMessage with up to8 unique fields, tags1..15 and uint32 values. Wire keys are(field<<3)|0 and all values use minimal base128 uint32 varints. Fields1 and2 are required; field1 is an ID and field2 is a ZigZag-encoded signed delta; unknown advisory3..15 fields are retained semantically. Reject duplicates, other wire types, critical tags>=16, overlong/nonminimal/truncated/overflow varints, missing required fields and excessive field count. Decode accepts any field order; encode sorts tags, preserves unknown values and publishes bytes/length only on success. This is a stated teaching subset, not general Protocol Buffers compatibility.',
 'For five-byte uint32 varints require the final payload<=15 and no continuation. Derive ZigZag using unsigned conversion/shift and a sign mask; decode odd values as -(value>>1)-1 to include INT32_MIN safely. Prove canonical decode/reencode idempotence and uniqueness for the admitted semantic map. Explain the tradeoff of rejecting duplicate fields compared with protocols that merge repeated occurrences.',
 'Bytes18 07 10 01 08 96 01 (hex) decode and canonicalize to08 96 01 10 01 18 07. 08 80 00 10 01 is rejected as nonminimal. ZigZag(-1)=1, INT32_MIN maps to UINT32_MAX, INT32_MAX maps to UINT32_MAX-1.',
 'Run replicated.wire and an independent Python parser/encoder for boundary values and reordered field maps. Kill overlong-varint acceptance and incorrect negative ZigZag mutants. Enumerate every truncation, duplicate field, forbidden tag/wire type and output-capacity boundary; demonstrate two noncanonical orders producing the same canonical bytes.',
 'Add length-delimited nested records with depth and total-byte budgets, a critical/advisory negotiation rule and an incremental io.frame decoder. Compare schema migration that retains unknown fields against field-wise reconstruction that loses them. Define canonical NaN/negative-zero/string normalization policy before adding floating or textual values; do not assume the current integer proof extends.',
 'Parsing uses a staged message; every varint advances only a local cursor until the whole message is accepted. Canonical encoding validates all tags and duplicates before writing a staged48byte buffer. Retaining semantic unknown integer values preserves their canonical meaning but not the original noncanonical byte order. Native struct layout and source language serialization defaults are irrelevant to the specified wire identity.',
 ['Separate key varint validation from field-number admission.','Avoid left shifting a negative signed integer.','A serializer can be deterministic within one build without being canonical across versions.'],
 [('minimal varint','The shortest base128 continuation encoding of an admitted unsigned integer.','parsable varint','A decoder can accept several byte strings for the same number.'),('canonical field-map encoding','One byte sequence selected by explicit ordering, numeric and duplicate rules for a semantic field map.','implementation determinism','Repeated output from one binary need not be stable across implementations or versions.')],
 'Eightinlinefieldpairs,48byte staged output; parsing O(bytes), simple tag-scan encoder O(15*8), no allocation. Unknown field retention consumes bounded slots. Wire compactness and CPU branches must be measured against a fixed-width alternative with identical validation.', ['codec.records','io.frame','testing.faults']),
card('Pure reducers, explicit effects and rollback replay','replay',['D14','deterministic-lockstep','rollback-netcode'],['ROLLBACK','EVENTS'],
 'Implement the T20.03 functional-core boundary: explicit state and recorded input produce new state and effect requests, while an adapter owns effects. Demonstrate rollback equivalence without replaying externally visible effects or claiming that ordinary C enforces purity.',
 'Implement RPState{tick0..8,position0..15,score0..65535,random:uint32}. rp_step consumes movement-1/0/1, wraps position on a16cell ring, advances LCG random=random*1664525+1013904223 modulo2^32, adds(random&3) with checked score limit and returns RPEffect{tick,score} as data. No clocks,I/O,allocation or callbacks occur in the reducer. rp_replay stages at most8 steps and effects, preserving outputs on failure. Restore the state before a changed input and replay the suffix; compare with a fresh fold of corrected inputs. rp_checkpoint composes two actual HAMT updates for position/score under one root publication and injects failure at each of6allocation ordinals; it is a projection checkpoint, not the full replay snapshot (tick/random remain in the explicit RPState snapshot).',
 'Prove determinism from complete state/input and defined unsigned arithmetic. Identify every effect boundary and distinguish speculative requests from committed presentation. Derive rollback of the HAMT used cursor after a failed second update while preserving old reachable roots; staged unreachable arena cells and allocation counters may differ. All arena users must be quiescent during the transaction, and roots from unpublished intermediate updates must never escape. Score overflow or invalid movement must not partially publish a state/effect.',
 'Initial(0,0,0,1),movement-1 gives position15,random1015568748,score0; next movement1 gives position0,score3. Eight inputs[1,1,0,-1,1,0,1,-1] produce score12. Replacing input4 with1 and replaying from the prior snapshot equals a fresh corrected fold. All six checkpoint failure cuts preserve the published root and used count.',
 'Run replicated.replay and exhaustively compare all movement histories of length0..8 with a Python integer model; verify suffix rollback versus full replay. Kill omitted random-score contribution and failed second-update allocation-leak mutants. Build a synthetic effect adapter with idempotency key(timeline,tick) and injected failure; record one duplicate-effect counterexample if replay directly invokes the adapter. Inspect undefined symbols to confirm the core has no time/random/I/O dependency.',
 'Introduce two local simulated players, predicted remote inputs and delayed corrections using ipc.local/io.frame, with bounded rollback horizon and retained snapshots. Separate authoritative committed effects from speculative presentation and define replacement/cancellation semantics. Reuse the actual T20.03 learner reducer adapter and storage.pages snapshot backend, then vary compiler/endianness and compare canonical state hashes. Implement a hidden wall-clock/random dependency as a deliberately failing counterexample, not a production change.',
 'The reducer takes state by value and returns effect data; C permits hidden globals but the contract and dependency audit forbid them. Replaying a suffix is equivalent to folding corrected inputs only if the checkpoint includes every influencing state field. HAMT root persistence alone omits PRNG/tick and therefore is deliberately not sufficient as a full game snapshot. Two updates need an outer allocation transaction; publishing the first root before the second succeeds breaks failure atomicity.',
 ['List every bit that can influence the next state.','Retain effects as data until the adapter chooses a commit policy.','A pure core helps replay; it does not make a failing effect adapter transactional.'],
 [('replay state completeness','The snapshot contains every value that can influence future core transitions under recorded inputs.','visible state snapshot','Rendered position alone can omit random, timing or protocol state.'),('effect request value','Data describing an intended external action returned by a pure transition.','performed effect','A returned request has not yet succeeded, failed or acquired external ownership.')],
 'Four scalar state fields, bounded8effect staging and imported HAMT arena; replay O(horizon), each checkpoint6clonednodes, retained roots pin storage. No core dynamic allocation or system calls; adapter latency, duplicated speculation and retained snapshot costs are separate dimensions.', ['persist.hamt','storage.pages','codec.records','io.frame','ipc.local','testing.faults']),
]

if __name__=='__main__':
    materialize(DATA)
